<!-- Application-level retints of names the framework's own control dictionaries read. Nothing here is an Astra
     resource key: every row exists because 26.10.9 paints something with a name of its own, that paint is wrong for
     a Fluent surface, and the markup that reads it cannot be replaced. A control template that owns the surface can
     be re-templated and reads Astra rows; a template that cannot be touched can only be reached through its name.
     That is the same mechanism the window-chrome and flyout-presenter aliases use (audits/window-shell.md,
     flyout-presenter.md), and the reason this dictionary is deliberately out of the key-consumption gate in
     tests/FluentJalium.Tests/Resources/AstraResourceKeyTests.cs: the reader lives in the framework, so there is no
     setter of ours that could cite it, and a gate that demanded one would only be satisfiable by a lie.
     What each row must keep doing, per row, is pinned by an Assert.Same fact in the control batch that found it. -->
<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.jalium.ui/2024" xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
  <!-- Found by the DataGrid batch. The runtime's DataGridRow template fills a selected row with this name, and its
       value on 26.10.9 is a brand-emerald LinearGradientBrush (#1D733C..#2B804A as measured,
       s1i-datagrid-tokens-raw.txt §J) - the exact colour this library is not allowed to show. The row cannot be
       re-templated to hold a different fill: installing a DataGridRow style strands the cell's content visual, so
       the cell renders no text at all (the reading is recorded in Styles/DataGrid.jalxaml). Aliasing rather than
       redefining is what makes this follow the application accent - both names resolve to the same brush instance,
       which is the instance FluentThemeManager.ApplyAccent and OverrideBrush move.
       Scope, measured over the framework's own dictionaries: 58 reads in twelve files (RangeControls 26, Dialogs 8,
       PropertyGrid 6, Calendar 5, ToggleControls 4, Containers 2, Navigation 2, TreeView 2, DataGrid 1,
       TreeDataGrid 1, DockLayout 1, MenusToolbars 1). Every one of them paints emerald today, so all of them change
       together. Controls this library restyles outright are unaffected - their own templates never ask for this
       name - which is what AstraDataGridTests.The_selected_row_fills_through_the_frameworks_own_name pins for the
       grid, and what the palette drift gate covers for the rest. -->
  <StaticResource x:Key="AccentBrush" ResourceKey="AccentFillColorDefaultBrush" />

  <!-- Found by the A2 alias-layer batch. The runtime paints a focused control's outer border with this name, and on
       26.10.9 the value it falls back to is brand green (ThemeColors.ControlBorderFocused is an expression-bodied
       property returning Color.FromRgb(32,114,69); #FF1E793F measured off the grid host's PART_OuterBorder under the
       IsKeyboardFocused cell, s1i-datagrid-tokens-raw.txt §J) - the colour this library is not allowed to show.
       The lever was measured on the authority rather than inherited from the reference tree: the six text families
       (TextBox, PasswordBox, NumberBox, AutoCompleteBox, DatePicker, TimePicker) resolve the name per call with the
       static only as the ?? fallback, which is what
       tests/FluentJalium.Tests/AstraFrameworkNameResolutionTests.cs pins with an installed probe brush.
       Solid, not upstream's gradient, deliberately: WinUI's twin for the same construct is
       TextControlElevationBorderFocusedBrush, a LinearGradientBrush (TextBox_themeresources.xaml:57/101/164 at
       19e3bdc, stops SystemAccentColorLight2 / Dark1 over ControlStrokeColorDefault), and a gradient parked behind an
       alias row freezes on the Light stops because FluentThemeManager.RefreshPalette re-tints SolidColorBrush
       instances in place. ThemeResources/TextBox.jalxaml:21-33 already recorded that reasoning and made the same
       substitution for our own row (TextControlBorderBrushFocused -> AccentFillColorDefaultBrush); this line extends
       that one decision to the name the framework reads, so the elevation ring is a documented deviation in exactly
       one place rather than two. Controls this library restyles are unaffected - their templates ask for Astra rows. -->
  <StaticResource x:Key="ControlBorderFocused" ResourceKey="AccentFillColorDefaultBrush" />

  <!-- Second segment of the same layer, chosen from the gap table in docs/astra/ROADMAP.md (#12) rather than from a
       name list: these two framework names carry the runtime's own iOS-flavoured grey set, which reads as a seam
       against the surfaces this library paints. Measured on 26.10.9 - SurfaceBackground #FFFFFFFF (Light) /
       #FF2C2C2E (Dark) against our SolidBackgroundFillColorBaseBrush #FFF3F3F3 / #FF202020; ControlBorder #FFD2D2D7 /
       #FF48484A against our ControlStrokeColorDefaultBrush #0F000000 / #12FFFFFF. The framework reads both names in
       code as well as in its own dictionaries (6 and 7 name lookups plus 18 and 8 markup reads), and every one of
       those sites is a surface this library cannot re-template - popups and application-level fills - so the name is
       the only lever. What these rows must NOT do is freeze: a StaticResource alias forwards the instance the palette
       holds, and the theme flip re-tints SolidColorBrush instances in place (Themes/FluentThemeManager.cs
       RefreshPalette), which is why the gradient behind an alias is a documented deviation above. That property is
       asserted, not assumed, by AstraFrameworkNameResolutionTests.A_retint_row_follows_the_theme_flip_to_the_variant_it_declares. -->
  <StaticResource x:Key="SurfaceBackground" ResourceKey="SolidBackgroundFillColorBaseBrush" />
  <StaticResource x:Key="ControlBorder" ResourceKey="ControlStrokeColorDefaultBrush" />

  <!-- Third segment of the same layer, and the first row here whose reader is proven at the PIXEL rather than at the
       dictionary or the reflection log. Instrument: install a probe brush under the name in Application.Resources,
       capture, count colours. Measured on 26.10.9 - a MenuFlyoutSubItem paints its own row text from TextSecondary
       (38 pixels of the framework grey become 38 pixels of the probe, and come back exactly when the probe is
       removed), and a native Label, which this library never restyles at all, resolves its Foreground from that same
       name. Probes under TextPrimary and TextDisabled move neither surface, which is why this is the one of the three
       that earns a row now and not the other two. Alias rather than redefine, for the standing reason: the palette
       twin TextFillColorSecondaryBrush carries the WinUI literal (#9E000000 Light / #C5FFFFFF Dark, translucent)
       where the framework's own value is an opaque macOS-flavoured grey (#FF6E6E73 / #FFD1D1D6), so forwarding the
       palette instance moves the surface toward 1:1 and keeps it following ApplyAccent and RefreshPalette. The rows
       this reaches are exactly the ones no template of ours can: the row's text is the control's own OnRender output
       (docs/astra/audits/menus.md, the ghost batch), and a Label has no style of ours at all. Pinned by
       AstraFrameworkNameResolutionTests.The_flyout_rows_own_text_is_painted_from_the_secondary_text_name and
       A_native_label_resolves_its_ink_from_the_same_name. What the row cost is the same evidence read the other way:
       the menu batch had a fact asserting our palette override reaches NEITHER the row's text nor its rule. The text
       half inverted - an override of this palette row now inks the control's own paint - so that fact is renamed and
       re-pinned onto the composited token value (AstraMenuTests.The_alias_layer_reaches_the_rows_own_text_while_
       our_rows_still_reach_the_rule), and its theme-flip sibling now captures each variant on an opaque plate because
       the token, unlike the grey it replaced, is translucent. -->
  <StaticResource x:Key="TextSecondary" ResourceKey="TextFillColorSecondaryBrush" />

  <!-- Fifth segment, and the weakest-evidenced row in this file: keep reading before reusing its shape.
       The reader is a self-drawn one - MenuItem never instantiates the template it stores, so its ink is decided by
       Jalium.UI.Controls' own per-call lookup (docs/astra/audits/menus.md), and the only lever over that ink is the
       name. Measured on 26.10.9 with a probe brush installed under TextPrimary: MenuItem.ResolvePrimaryTextBrush
       returns the probe (AstraFrameworkNameResolutionTests.A_self_drawn_menu_item_reads_the_primary_text_name), so the
       lookup is live rather than cached, which is the precondition this layer has used for every row.
       What is NOT measured is a pixel. The TextSecondary row above was proven by counting colours in a capture;
       this one is proven by reading a resolver's return value, and every attempt to photograph a built MenuItem
       came back uniform #000000 - the same wall #50 documents for glyph ink. So this row buys name-resolution-level
       purity for a surface whose pixels this test bed cannot see yet, and it is written down as such rather than
       described as a visual fix. Alias, not redefine, for the standing reason: the two names resolve to one instance,
       which is the instance FluentThemeManager.ApplyAccent, OverrideBrush and RefreshPalette move.
       Solidity: TextFillColorPrimaryBrush is a SolidColorBrush in both variants (#E4000000 / #FFFFFFFF), so the
       freeze-on-a-gradient case documented above does not apply here. -->
  <StaticResource x:Key="TextPrimary" ResourceKey="TextFillColorPrimaryBrush" />

  <!-- Sixth segment. The question this row answers is narrower than "does anything read the name" and was the reason
       it sat unshipped one round longer: the framework's disabled ink reads #FFAEAEB2, which is EXACTLY the value the
       name TextDisabled projects to, so the colour alone cannot tell a by-name lookup from a direct ThemeColors read
       - and only the first of those two is something this layer can redirect. Measured on 26.10.9 by installing a
       probe under the name and toggling IsEnabled, on the four surfaces whose labels this library re-templates but
       never owns the text element of: CheckBox, RadioButton, ListBoxItem and ComboBoxItem. Each generated label
       reads resting #E4000000 (ours) while enabled, and with the probe installed its disabled stamp becomes
       #FF112233 - the probe INSTANCE, not just the colour - then returns to #FFAEAEB2 when the probe is removed.
       So the stamp is a local value on that element (which is why no cell of ours could ever reach it, the reading
       #51/#55 recorded) but the VALUE it stamps is looked up by name at stamp time, and the name is this row's
       property. Alias, not redefine: TextFillColorDisabledBrush carries the WinUI literal (#5C000000 / #5DFFFFFF,
       translucent) where the framework's projection is an opaque macOS-flavoured grey (#FFAEAEB2 / #FF636366), so
       forwarding the palette instance moves four real surfaces toward 1:1 and keeps them following ApplyAccent and
       RefreshPalette. Two legs of the same measurement came back empty and are recorded as empty rather than as
       support: a disabled TextBox offers no generated TextBlock to read at all (its ink lives in the framework's
       text host), and a disabled DataGridColumnHeader already paints our own #5C000000 - our style row wins there, so
       the framework never stamps it. No pixel witness exists either: a disabled MenuFlyoutItem's crop is page colour
       edge to edge (0 pixels of the grey, 0 of the probe), the #50 wall again, so this row's grade is
       stamp-value-level, like TextPrimary's is, not like TextSecondary's. The blast radius is by design and was
       enumerated by running the suite rather than by grepping literals: every fact that pinned "the framework owns
       the disabled label colour" flips to ours, which is that row landing, not a regression. -->
  <StaticResource x:Key="TextDisabled" ResourceKey="TextFillColorDisabledBrush" />
</ResourceDictionary>
